Round 5 (monorepo report "Round 5 analysis"): the separate `styled-ppx.descendant`
tier is gone. It sat below `styled-ppx.base` unconditionally, so an ancestor's
descendant-shaped rule (see `is_descendant_shape` - a combinator reaching for
an unclassed subject: a bare element type, `*`, or a pseudo-class chain with
no class of its own) always lost to a child's own atom regardless of
specificity - fixing the one real tie (`/gbp-monitor` below) by breaking five
more patterns where the ancestor's rule is genuinely MORE specific and should
win, same as it did before tiers existed. Now a descendant-shaped rule stays
in the tier of its own CONTEXT - `conditional` if wrapped in `@media`/
`@supports`/`@container` or carrying a pseudo-class directly on its own
compound, `base` otherwise - exactly like any other rule, so real
specificity decides, whichever of the two a rule lands in (`base` and
`conditional` share the one `styled-ppx.atoms` layer - see "Round 7b" at
the end of this file for a conditional-vs-base pair specificity has to
decide between; a `[%styled.global]` rule is a separate case, its own
lower `styled-ppx.global` layer, see `layers-global-tier.t`). Within
whichever tier it lands in, a descendant-shaped rule is still emitted
BEFORE own-element rules (the shorthand-before-longhand sort's new primary
key), so the one case that needed the old tier - a genuine specificity TIE -
still resolves to the child, since the later rule in a tie wins.

`/gbp-monitor` (monorepo report "Round 3 analysis", cause #4): a wrapper's
descendant rule (`._in_j8vuse * { color }`, a bundle - no class of its own on
the subject, a bare `*`) and the span's own class (`._a_4esjgz { color }`)
are genuinely TIED: one class selector each ((0,1,0) - `*` and the wrapper's
own class both contribute nothing beyond the one class), so without
something to break the tie the browser applies whichever is textually LAST.

  $ cat > gbp.ml <<EOF
  > [@@@css "._a_4esjgz{color:black;}"]
  > [@@@css "._in_j8vuse * {color:white;}"]
  > EOF

  $ styled-ppx.generate gbp.ml
  /* This file is generated by styled-ppx, do not edit manually */
  @layer styled-ppx.global, styled-ppx.atoms;
  @layer styled-ppx.atoms {
  ._in_j8vuse * {color:white;}
  ._a_4esjgz{color:black;}
  }

What the browser does now, and why: both rules are unconditional and neither
carries a pseudo-class of its own, so both are classified `base` and land in
the same `styled-ppx.atoms` layer - no tier-order tiebreak available, same as
before tiers existed. The descendant-shaped wrapper rule is emitted FIRST
(the sort's own primary key, unrelated to tier order),
so on the (0,1,0) vs (0,1,0) tie the span's own `color:black`, being LATER,
wins - restoring "an element's own class beats an ancestor's blind reach for
`*`/an unclassed descendant" without needing a dedicated lower layer for it.

A subject WITH its own class - `.tiptap` here, or an `.id-...` class a
`$(binding)` reference would resolve to - is not this shape: it targets a
specific, identified element, not "whatever happens to be under here", so it
is not reordered and stays wherever the shorthand-sort (or plain source
order) would otherwise put it.

  $ cat > classed_descendant.ml <<EOF
  > [@@@css "._a_wrap9k .tiptap{color:navy;}"]
  > EOF

  $ styled-ppx.generate classed_descendant.ml
  /* This file is generated by styled-ppx, do not edit manually */
  @layer styled-ppx.global, styled-ppx.atoms;
  @layer styled-ppx.atoms {
  ._a_wrap9k .tiptap{color:navy;}
  }

Round 5 pattern: `.list li { display: grid }`. The wrapper's rule
(`._a_listwrap li`, one class + one type selector, specificity (0,1,1)) is
MORE specific than the li's own atom (`._a_liblock`, (0,1,0)) - real CSS
specificity, not stylesheet position, decides once both share the same
`styled-ppx.atoms` layer, so the wrapper wins regardless of which one the
sort emits first (unlike the tied `/gbp-monitor` case above).

  $ cat > list_li.ml <<EOF
  > [@@@css "._a_listwrap li{display:grid;}"]
  > [@@@css "._a_liblock{display:block;}"]
  > EOF

  $ styled-ppx.generate list_li.ml
  /* This file is generated by styled-ppx, do not edit manually */
  @layer styled-ppx.global, styled-ppx.atoms;
  @layer styled-ppx.atoms {
  ._a_listwrap li{display:grid;}
  ._a_liblock{display:block;}
  }

