Round 5 pattern: `@media (min-width:1280px) { .menu > :last-child {
margin-right: 0 } }` versus the item's own, unconditional `margin-right`
atom. The wrapper's rule is wrapped in `@media`, so it is classified
`conditional`; the item's own atom is unconditional, so it is `base`. Both
share the one `styled-ppx.atoms` layer, so this IS a specificity question:
`._a_menuwrap > :last-child` is one class plus one pseudo-class, (0,1,1),
strictly more specific than the plain atom's (0,1,0) - the wrapper wins at
that breakpoint by specificity, same as it would with no tiers at all;
`generate.ml` also emits `conditional`-tier rules after `base`-tier ones
(see "Cascade tiers" in `generate.ml`), which would settle a genuine tie
the same way, but specificity alone already decides this pair.

  $ cat > media_menu.ml <<EOF
  > [@@@css "@media (min-width:1280px){._a_menuwrap > :last-child{margin-right:0;}}"]
  > [@@@css "._a_marginright{margin-right:24px;}"]
  > EOF

  $ styled-ppx.generate media_menu.ml
  /* This file is generated by styled-ppx, do not edit manually */
  @layer styled-ppx.global, styled-ppx.atoms;
  @layer styled-ppx.atoms {
  ._a_marginright{margin-right:24px;}
  @media (min-width:1280px){._a_menuwrap > :last-child{margin-right:0;}}
  }

Round 5 pattern: `.sameLineBadge > :first-child:not(:last-child) { display:
inline }`. The wrapper's rule is `.sameLineBadge` (one class) plus
`:first-child` plus `:not(:last-child)` (a negation pseudo-class's own
specificity is its argument's - here another pseudo-class), so three
pseudo-class-equivalents: specificity (0,3,0). The badge's own atom
(`._a_displaydesktop`) is (0,1,0). The wrapper is more specific, so it wins;
both unconditional, both classified `base`, both in the one
`styled-ppx.atoms` layer.

  $ cat > same_line_badge.ml <<EOF
  > [@@@css "._a_badgewrap > :first-child:not(:last-child){display:inline;}"]
  > [@@@css "._a_displaydesktop{display:block;}"]
  > EOF

  $ styled-ppx.generate same_line_badge.ml
  /* This file is generated by styled-ppx, do not edit manually */
  @layer styled-ppx.global, styled-ppx.atoms;
  @layer styled-ppx.atoms {
  ._a_badgewrap > :first-child:not(:last-child){display:inline;}
  ._a_displaydesktop{display:block;}
  }

Round 7b pattern (`sameLineBadge`, monorepo report "Round 7b"): the round-5
pattern above, but the badge's own override is narrower under
`@media` instead of unconditional: `._a_ancestor > :first-child:not
(:last-child) { display: inline }` (0,3,0, `base` - unconditional, no
pseudo-class on the wrapper's OWN compound) versus `@media
(max-width:1000px) { ._a_badgeblock { display: block } }` (0,1,0,
`conditional`). Round 7 (three SEPARATE CSS layers, `styled-ppx.base` <
`styled-ppx.conditional`) made every `conditional` rule outrank every
`base` rule regardless of specificity - so this less specific, narrower
override incorrectly beat the more specific ancestor rule at every
viewport width, making the badge `block` on narrow screens when it should
stay `inline` (163 of 167 monorepo pages differed at 375/1000px). This is
the real bug "Cascade tiers" in `generate.ml` (above) exists to fix:
putting `base` and `conditional` in the SAME `styled-ppx.atoms` layer
restores specificity between them, exactly like the
`media_menu.ml`/`same_line_badge.ml` cases above - the only difference
from `media_menu.ml` is which SIDE is more specific.

  $ cat > same_line_badge_media.ml <<EOF
  > [@@@css "._a_ancestor > :first-child:not(:last-child){display:inline;}"]
  > [@@@css "@media (max-width:1000px){._a_badgeblock{display:block;}}"]
  > EOF

  $ styled-ppx.generate same_line_badge_media.ml
  /* This file is generated by styled-ppx, do not edit manually */
  @layer styled-ppx.global, styled-ppx.atoms;
  @layer styled-ppx.atoms {
  ._a_ancestor > :first-child:not(:last-child){display:inline;}
  @media (max-width:1000px){._a_badgeblock{display:block;}}
  }

What the browser does now, and why: both rules share the one
`styled-ppx.atoms` layer, so specificity is compared directly - (0,3,0)
beats (0,1,0),
whatever the viewport width and regardless of which rule is textually
later. The ancestor's `display:inline` wins even under 1000px, where the
media query matches and the round-7 design would have (wrongly) let the
less specific `._a_badgeblock` win by tier/layer order alone.
